- Manager training budget covering all people managers globally;
- Technology investment for the approved collaboration stack and meeting-room upgrades;
- Real-estate budget for purpose-based space conversions in pilot locations.

## Recommended position

The company should not attempt to predict one permanent answer for where employees work. It should build an operating system that allows the answer to evolve.

> **Standardize the conditions for effective work, not the location of every employee. Give teams clear outcomes and boundaries, make offices worth visiting, design digital collaboration intentionally, and allow local cultures and legal realities to inform the final shape.**

The framework proposed here is deliberately designed to be adaptable. It sets global principles where consistency matters—fairness, security, performance, and inclusion—while delegating implementation to local teams where context matters. It treats flexibility as a business capability, not merely an employee benefit. It measures outcomes and equity, not attendance. And it builds in regular review cycles so the company can learn, adjust, and improve as conditions change.

The cost of getting this wrong is not just employee dissatisfaction. It is the risk of losing talent, falling behind in innovation, creating legal exposure, and building a culture where presence matters more than contribution. The cost of getting it right is a more resilient, more inclusive, and more effective organization—one that can attract and retain the best people, wherever they are, and help them do their best work, however they work.
